---
source: src/main.rs
expression: "Args::command().render_long_help().to_string()"
---
A simple, clean serial monitor with a clean terminal UI for embedded development

Usage: sermonizer [OPTIONS]

Options:
  -p, --port <PORT>
          Serial port path/name (auto-detect if omitted)

  -b, --baud <BAUD>
          Baud rate
          
          [default: 115200]

      --line-ending <LINE_ENDING>
          Line ending when you press Enter (none|nl|cr|crlf; lf is an alias for nl). Default: nl

          Possible values:
          - none: Send nothing extra (no line ending)
          - nl:   Send '\n' (LF)
          - cr:   Send '\r' (CR)
          - crlf: Send "\r\n" (CRLF)

      --data-bits <DATA_BITS>
          Data bits per character
          
          [default: 8]
          [possible values: 5, 6, 7, 8]

      --parity <PARITY>
          Parity checking mode
          
          [default: none]
          [possible values: none, odd, even]

      --stop-bits <STOP_BITS>
          Stop bits
          
          [default: 1]
          [possible values: 1, 2]

      --flow-control <FLOW_CONTROL>
          Flow control mode
          
          [default: none]
          [possible values: none, software, hardware]

      --dtr <DTR>
          Set the DTR line after opening (left untouched if omitted)
          
          [possible values: on, off]

      --rts <RTS>
          Set the RTS line after opening (left untouched if omitted)
          
          [possible values: on, off]

      --log <LOG>
          Log received bytes to this file (appends)

      --tx-log <TX_LOG>
          Log transmitted bytes to this file (appends)

  -t, --timestamps
          Prepend timestamps to displayed and logged lines

      --hex
          Show RX as hex (space-separated bytes)

      --raw
          Keep ANSI escape sequences in the display instead of stripping them

  -e, --echo
          Show what you send in the output, prefixed with "> "

  -w, --wrap
          Wrap long lines instead of clipping them (toggle at runtime with Ctrl+T)

      --mouse
          Scroll the output with the mouse wheel (the terminal then needs Shift+drag to select text)

      --list
          Just list ports and exit

      --all-ports
          Also list and offer ports of unknown type, such as onboard UARTs (/dev/ttyS*), which are hidden by default

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version
